              <td>Iddah</td>
              <td>None (Art. 82)</td>
              <td>Three periods of purity or three lunar months (Arts. 82, 84); until birth if pregnant (Art. 85)</td>
              <td>Only if there was seclusion or intercourse (Art. 82)</td>
              <td>None (Art. 82)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Iddah maintenance</td>
              <td>None</td>
              <td>Yes, after a revocable divorce (Art. 101)</td>
              <td>None after khula (Art. 101)</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mut&rsquo;a (Art. 102)</td>
              <td>No: the article covers a consummated marriage</td>
              <td>Possible if he divorced by his sole will, without her request or cause</td>
              <td>No: the separation is at her request</td>
              <td>No: before consummation and at her request</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Take-back (ruju)</td>
              <td>No: minor irrevocable (Art. 60(2))</td>
              <td>Yes, during the iddah (Art. 61)</td>
              <td>No: minor irrevocable (Art. 65)</td>
              <td>No: the contract is revoked</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Remarrying each other</td>
              <td>New contract and new mahr (Art. 59(2)(a))</td>
              <td>Take-back during the iddah; after it, new contract and new mahr</td>
              <td>New contract and new mahr (Art. 59(2)(a))</td>
              <td>A new contract, which carries its own dowry (Art. 47(1))</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Marrying someone else</td>
              <td>Immediately</td>
              <td>After the iddah ends (Art. 83)</td>
              <td>Immediately if there was no seclusion or intercourse; otherwise after the iddah</td>
              <td>Immediately once the judgment is final</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Articles of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, assuming a first or second divorce; a third is major
          irrevocable (Art. 59(2)(b)). Article 102 speaks of a consummated marriage, so with seclusion only,
          take advice before counting on mut&rsquo;a.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Article 75 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="article75-heading">
      <h2 id="article75-heading">Can a Wife End the Marriage Before Consummation Without His Agreement?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Under Article 75 of the 2024 law, if the wife asks for revocation before intercourse or
        seclusion and the husband refuses to divorce her or agree khula, the court shall revoke the marriage
        contract, provided she returns the dowry she received and reconciliation is impossible. She does not
        have to prove harm.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>The court must act once the conditions are met.</strong> It says &ldquo;shall revoke&rdquo;, not &ldquo;may&rdquo;.</li>
        <li><strong>No harm to prove.</strong> Compare <a href="/en/divorce-for-harm-uae/">divorce for harm</a> under Article 71, which is built around showing harm, and contested discord, which can go to arbitrators for up to 60 days (Article 72).</li>
        <li><strong>A possible refund of wedding costs.</strong> If her reason relates to her, she must return, on his request, the dowry and what he spent at her request for the marriage (Article 75(2)).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Article 75 or khula?</h3>
      <p>
        Khula is separation at the wife&rsquo;s request with the husband accepting a consideration, taking
        effect as a minor irrevocable divorce (Article 65). If the consideration is the dowry, giving back
        what she received is enough and the rest is dropped, and if he stubbornly refuses, the court can
        order khula for an appropriate consideration (Article 66). Before consummation both routes end with
        her giving up the dowry and leaving without an iddah. The difference: Article 75 fixes the price at
        the dowry received, plus wedding spending only where the reason relates to her, while in a contested
        khula the court decides the consideration. See our <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>How an Article 75 case usually runs</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Check the facts</h3>
          <p>Confirm there was no intercourse and no time alone that could be argued as seclusion. If there was, compare khula and divorce for harm instead.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Choose the court</h3>
          <p>A wife&rsquo;s revocation case can be filed where she or the husband lives or works, or where the marital home is (Article 3). If he has left the UAE, see Article 4 below.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>File the claim</h3>
          <p>Attach the marriage contract (attested if issued abroad), ID documents and proof of the dowry paid. Claims can be filed electronically or on paper (Article 44 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Reconciliation is tested</h3>
          <p>The judge may refer you to Family Guidance if a settlement looks feasible; the referral is discretionary (Article 8). Article 75 requires reconciliation to be impossible, so expect the question.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Return the dowry, keep the receipts</h3>
          <p>If he claims wedding costs under Article 75(2), proof of who asked for what decides the amount.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Judgment and appeal</h3>
          <p>Either side may appeal within 30 days (Article 9). There is no iddah, so once the judgment is final she can remarry.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Defects -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="defect-heading">
      <h2 id="defect-heading">Non-Consummation Because of Impotence or Another Medical Condition</h2>
      <p>
        A condition that prevents intimacy is a ground to end the marriage, and the wife&rsquo;s right to rely
        on it never lapses. Article 69 lets either spouse ask the court to revoke the contract for a serious,
        harmful defect, or for one that prevents sexual pleasure, such as impotence, whether it existed
        before the contract or arose after it.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Knowledge usually matters, but not for her.</strong> The right is normally lost if the spouse knew of the defect before the contract or accepted it afterwards, but the wife&rsquo;s right for a defect that prevents sexual pleasure is never extinguished.</li>
        <li><strong>Incurable:</strong> the court revokes without a grace period.</li>
        <li><strong>Curable:</strong> the case is postponed for up to one year; if it is not cured and the applicant insists, the court revokes.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> the court may use experts, in practice medical examination and reports.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The dowry depends on timing and on whose defect it was (Article 70). If the defect existed before
        the contract and the marriage is revoked before intercourse or seclusion, the husband recovers what
        he paid of the dowry and the rest falls away (Article 70(2)). If the husband knew of the wife&rsquo;s
        defect before the contract and still asks for revocation for it before intercourse and seclusion,
        she receives half the dowry (Article 70(4)). Other paragraphs cover revocation after intercourse.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many couples in this position have lived together, so there has been seclusion even without
        intercourse. That takes the case outside the before-seclusion paragraphs, and the dowry outcome needs
        a lawyer&rsquo;s reading of the full Article 70. Talaq and khula stay open too.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Refusal to move in -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="refusal-heading">
      <h2 id="refusal-heading">When One Spouse Will Not Move In or Consummate</h2>
      <p>
        A wife may lawfully refuse to move into the marital home until she receives her due dowry and a
        suitable home (Article 50). That answers the common complaint that the bride &ldquo;refuses the
        rukhsati&rdquo;: if the prompt mahr is unpaid or no proper home is ready, waiting is her right. If she
        consummates before receiving the dowry, it remains a debt she can claim at any time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once the dowry is paid and a suitable home is ready, the position changes. Under Article 103 a wife
        loses her right to maintenance if, without a legitimate excuse, she denies herself to her husband or
        refuses to move into the marital home. Loss of maintenance is the consequence the article names; it
        is not a divorce in itself.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>If the wife wants out</h3>
          <p>
            Article 75 revocation, khula (Articles 65 and 66), divorce for harm, revocation for a defect
            (Article 69), or, where a dowry that is due has not been paid, revocation for non-payment of the
            dowry (Article 76).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>If the husband wants out</h3>
          <p>
            Talaq, which before seclusion means paying half the named mahr (Article 47(3)), documented within
            15 days (Article 58). If he believes her refusal is unjustified, he should ask a lawyer whether a
            case for discord protects him better before he pronounces anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Gifts and wedding costs -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="gifts-heading">
      <h2 id="gifts-heading">Wedding Costs, Gold and Engagement Gifts</h2>
      <p>
        Three kinds of money change hands in a marriage that ends before the rukhsati, and the 2024 law
        treats them differently.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>The mahr.</strong> Halved on a talaq before consummation (Article 47(3)), returned under Article 75, or used as the consideration in khula (Article 66). Gold or cash counts as mahr if the contract or the evidence shows it was given as dowry.</li>
        <li><strong>Wedding spending at the wife&rsquo;s request.</strong> Hall bookings, furniture or other costs she or her family asked for. She repays these on his request when she seeks revocation before consummation for a reason relating to her (Article 75(2)).</li>
        <li><strong>Engagement gifts.</strong> The 2024 law has separate provisions on engagement and gifts. Whether a ring, jewellery or clothes go back depends on those provisions, what the gift was for and who ended things, so have a lawyer apply them to your list rather than relying on family custom.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Keep receipts, transfers and messages showing who paid for what and at whose request. The Umm Al
        Quwain repayment order above listed exactly these items.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Islamic background -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="islamic-heading">
      <h2 id="islamic-heading">Divorce Before Consummation in Islam: The Verses Behind the Rules</h2>
      <p>
        The UAE rules follow verses of the Qur&rsquo;an that address this situation directly, and the fatwas
        most people find online are built on the same verses.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Al-Baqarah 2:237:</strong> if a husband divorces his wife before touching her, having fixed a mahr, half of what was fixed is due, unless the wife forgoes it or the one who holds the marriage tie forgoes his share. The verse adds that forgoing is closer to righteousness.</li>
        <li><strong>Al-Baqarah 2:236:</strong> there is no blame in divorcing a wife before touching her or fixing a mahr, but she should be given a fair provision, the wealthy according to his means and the poor according to his.</li>
        <li><strong>Al-Ahzab 33:49:</strong> believers who marry and then divorce before touching their wives have no waiting period to count against them; they should provide for them and release them graciously.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The schools of Islamic law differ on one point that matters in practice: whether valid seclusion
        without intercourse has the same effect as consummation for the mahr and the waiting period. A UAE
        court does not have to choose a school, because the 2024 law answers it: proper seclusion confirms
        the full dowry (Article 47(2)), triggers the iddah (Article 82) and makes a first talaq revocable
        (Article 60). Where no mahr was named, the statute expresses her entitlement as no more than half of
        the usual dowry (Article 47(3)), which gives the court a measurable figure.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a religious ruling for your own conscience, ask a scholar you trust. For rights a UAE court will
        enforce, the 2024 law is the reference.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Expats -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="expat-heading">
      <h2 id="expat-heading">Nikah in Pakistan, India or Bangladesh, but Living in the UAE</h2>
      <p>
        If you live in the UAE, a UAE court can usually hear your case even though the nikah was signed
        abroad, and the 2024 law will usually govern it. Article 3 gives UAE courts jurisdiction over cases
        against foreigners with a domicile, residence or place of work here, and Article 1(3) applies the
        2024 law to non-UAE citizens unless one spouse insists on their own law or another agreed law, as
        permitted.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Both of you live here.</strong> A wife&rsquo;s divorce, khula or revocation case can go to the court where either of you lives or works, or where the marital home is (Article 3).</li>
        <li><strong>She lives here, he has gone home.</strong> Article 4(2) lets a resident wife file for divorce or revocation against a husband who lived or worked here and then left her and moved abroad, or whose address abroad is unknown. He can be served by email or messaging apps (Article 9 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022).</li>
        <li><strong>He lives here, she is abroad.</strong> She can sue in the UAE because he resides here (Article 3), but must name an address for service and a legal representative here (Article 44(2)(a) of the same code).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The court needs the nikah nama or marriage certificate, certified in the issuing country, legalised
        by the UAE embassy there and attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs, with a legal Arabic
        translation. The UAE is not a party to the Hague Apostille Convention, so an apostille alone is not
        accepted. Check the mahr entries: the nikah nama usually records the amount, the prompt and deferred
        split and anything paid at the ceremony, and those entries drive the half-mahr calculation.
      </p>
      <p>
        A UAE divorce also has to be registered or recognised back home under that country&rsquo;s procedure.
        For Pakistani nationals, see our guide to <a href="/en/pakistani-divorce-uae/">Pakistani divorce in
        the UAE</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Non-Muslims -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="nonmuslim-heading">
      <h2 id="nonmuslim-heading">Non-Muslim Couples: Consummation Does Not Affect a Civil Divorce</h2>
      <p>
        For non-Muslims, consummation makes no difference to getting divorced. Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of
        2022 applies to non-Muslim foreigners living in the UAE unless one of them adheres to the law of
        their home country (Article 1). Either spouse&rsquo;s wish to separate is enough, with no need to
        justify it or show harm (Article 7), and the court grants the divorce once the other spouse has been
        served (Article 8), without Family Guidance (Article 3). In Abu Dhabi, non-Muslims use the Abu Dhabi
        Civil Family Court under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether an unconsummated marriage can instead be annulled, as if it never existed, is a different
        question, answered by the law that governs the marriage, which may be home-country law. See
        <a href="/en/annulment-vs-divorce-uae/">annulment vs divorce</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-inline">
        <p>Not sure whether a meeting counted as seclusion, or what mahr is owed?</p>
        <a href="#contact" class="btn btn--primary">Ask for a Free Consultation</a>
      </div>
    </section>

